{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgahWJTQunjVwjvPPSPyo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I proved `myRev_myRev` in `Main.lean`, and `lean Main.lean` now runs with no errors or warnings.\n\nThe proof needed one extra lemma, which I added just above the theorem:\n\n```lean\ntheorem myRev_append (xs ys : List α) : myRev (xs ++ ys) = myRev ys ++ myRev xs := by\n  induction xs with\n  | nil => simp [myRev]\n  | cons x xs ih => simp [myRev, ih, List.append_assoc]\n\ntheorem myRev_myRev (xs : List α) : myRev (myRev xs) = xs := by\n  induction xs with\n  | nil => rfl\n  | cons x xs ih => simp [myRev, myRev_append, ih]\n```\n\n- **`myRev_append`**: reversing two joined lists gives the reversed second list followed by the reversed first. It's proved by induction on `xs`.\n- **`myRev_myRev`**: also by induction. In the `x :: xs` case, `myRev (myRev xs ++ [x])` becomes `myRev [x] ++ myRev (myRev xs)` by `myRev_append`. That simplifies to `[x] ++ xs`, using the induction hypothesis for the second part."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":658,"cache_read_input_tokens":7903,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":658},"output_tokens":408,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1acf0d47-373f-4d75-99a6-30dd1ee2451a","uuid":"4b38e834-2aac-44a8-a63f-4d846f20ad80","timestamp":"2026-10-31T17:20:42.700Z","request_id":"req_011CgahWJD3Qpo3L7fqiPgYc"}
